libHexEx uses the CMake build system (CMake >= 3.12).

Dependencies
------------
libHexEx depends on OpenVolumeMesh >= 3.4.1. Both dependencies are tracked as
git submodules in third_party/ and are pinned to known-good releases:

    third_party/OpenVolumeMesh   OpenVolumeMesh v3.4.1
    third_party/googletest       GoogleTest v1.17.0 (unit tests only)
    third_party/CLI11            CLI11 v2.7.2 (the hexex application only)

Fetch them with:

    git clone --recursive <libHexEx-url>
    # or, in an existing checkout:
    git submodule update --init --recursive

If the submodules are not checked out (i.e. third_party/ is empty), CMake falls
back to downloading the same versions via FetchContent, so a plain non-recursive
clone still configures - it just takes longer the first time.


Quick start (recommended, CMake >= 3.23)
----------------------------------------
    git submodule update --init --recursive
    cmake --preset debug          # configures into ./build
    cmake --build --preset debug  # builds lib, demos and tests
    ctest --preset debug          # runs the unit tests
    ./build/Build/bin/minimum_example

A Release preset is available as well:

    cmake --preset release
    cmake --build --preset release
    ctest --preset release

The presets are also picked up automatically by CLion and other IDEs that
support CMakePresets.json. Select the "debug" preset as the CMake profile.


Manual configure (without presets)
----------------------------------
    cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
    cmake --build build --parallel
    ctest --test-dir build


Using a different OpenVolumeMesh / googletest / CLI11
---------------------------------------------
Option A - point HexEx at another third_party directory:

    cmake -S . -B build -DHEXEX_THIRD_PARTY_DIR=/path/to/my/third_party

Option B - only for the FetchContent fallback, use local sources:

    cmake -S . -B build -DFETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_OVM=/path/to/OpenVolumeMesh

Option C - use an installed / pre-built OpenVolumeMesh, letting find_package
locate it (it must be found before HexEx is configured, e.g. by calling
find_package(OpenVolumeMesh REQUIRED) in the parent project):

    cmake -S . -B build -DOpenVolumeMesh_DIR=/path/to/OpenVolumeMesh/lib/cmake/OpenVolumeMesh

CLI11 can also be replaced in the same way
(-DHEXEX_THIRD_PARTY_DIR=...) or, if the submodule is missing, is downloaded via
FetchContent (tag v2.7.2).

Option D - add libHexEx to a parent project with add_subdirectory(); if the
parent already provides the OpenVolumeMesh::OpenVolumeMesh target (and, for the
tests, GTest::gtest_main), HexEx uses it and skips third_party entirely.


Options
-------
    HEXEX_BUILD_UNIT_TESTS   Build the unit tests (default: ON for a standalone
                             build, OFF when included in another project).
    HEXEX_WITH_GEOGRAM       Build the geogram bridge and the geogram output of
                             the hexex application: AUTO (detect geogram, the
                             default), ON (fail if geogram is missing) or OFF.
    GEOGRAM_ROOT             geogram source tree or installation (default:
                             $HOME/geogram if it exists).
    HEXEX_THIRD_PARTY_DIR    Where the dependency submodules live (default:
                             <source>/third_party).
    STL_RANGE_CHECKS         Enable libstdc++ debug-mode range checks in Debug
                             builds (default: OFF).


Notes
-----
* Output locations for a standalone build:
    libraries  -> build/Build/lib
    executables-> build/Build/bin (the unit test binary is build/Build/unittests)
* Install with: cmake --install build --prefix /path/to/prefix
  This also installs OpenVolumeMesh (headers, library and its CMake package
  config) and the HexEx CMake package config, so downstream projects can use
  find_package(HexEx) and link against HexEx::HexEx.
* The bundled exact predicates (Shewchuk) must not be compiled with x87
  extended precision. On x86 the CMakeLists therefore forces -msse
  -mfpmath=sse; compilation on ARM is unaffected.
* macOS: build with Apple Clang (the default). A Homebrew GCC is currently not
  usable, because its driver passes a deployment target to the linker that the
  installed Apple toolchain rejects ("invalid version number in
  '-mmacosx-version-min=...'").
